SWEET AKS: a lock tells SpringBoard the lock state changed, so the lock screen asks for the code (2326)

ast266, 2026-11-07: the owner set a code through Settings ("SWEET AKS: set -> ok"), the runner pressed the side
button, SpringBoard called selector 8 within 0.2 s and the kernel logged `lock -> ok` seven times. On the wake and
the swipe, the lock screen went straight into Settings with no keypad, with code 8361 live. This is the ast253
bypass again, in the third place it occurs. 2325 fixed the set, 2325b fixed the turn-off; a lock was still silent
in the form SpringBoard listens for.

The decision, from the 26.6.2 cache (full addresses in plans/passcode/lock3-decision-path.md):

  -[SBLockScreenManager lockScreenViewControllerDidPresent]'s block (0x224befa4c) shows the keypad only when
  -isAuthenticated (0x22517c240) is false and -hasPasscodeSet (0x225167700) is true. The has-passcode byte was
  already right after 2325. The failing half is isAuthenticated: -[SBFUserAuthenticationController isAuthenticated]
  (0x1c16a8424) tail-calls -_isUserAuthenticated (0x1c16a8428), which branches on _authState (+0x28). When that is
  Revoking (2) it does NOT read the lock state; it returns the grace-period assertion's validity
  (0x1c16a8474-0x1c16a856c). And -_revokeAuthenticationImmediately:forPublicReason: (0x1c170c568) -- the only caller
  of MKBLockDevice, and therefore of selector 8 -- ends with `_setAuthState: 2` (0x1c170c6f0) and creates that
  assertion (0x1c16ea1c8). A lock therefore pins SpringBoard in Revoking, and a successful selector 26 (the
  transient tracker's addAssertion:, 0x1c16eadc0 -> MKBDeviceLockAssertion -> aks_assert_hold) keeps the assertion
  valid, so isAuthenticated stays true for the rest of the boot.

  The way out of Revoking is -_updateAuthenticationStateAndDateForLockState: (0x1c170c1a0), which stores Revoked
  when isEffectivelyLocked is true (0x1c170c1f8). isEffectivelyLocked (0x1c16b0498) is the mask 0x46 over the MAPPED
  lockState, so sls 1 -> Locked -> true. The kernel's answer after a lock was already right. That method's only
  caller is -[SBFUserAuthenticationController keybag:extendedStateDidChange:] (0x1c16ec7fc), reached from
  SBFMobileKeyBag's status-change dispatch (0x1c16ec6e4), reached from -_queue_handleKeybagStatusChanged
  (0x1c16ec1c4), reached from the interest block (0x1c16ec08c) ONLY for AKSEvent 1:

    0x1c16ec0bc  cbz   w21, 0x1c16ec0ec    ; event 0: +0x31 and _queue_firstUnlockOccurred
    0x1c16ec0c0  cmp   w21, #1
    0x1c16ec0c4  b.ne  0x1c16ec104         ; everything else: return, no refresh

  Apple's aksNotificationCB (0x1e4ae45f4) maps 0xd00c -> 4, 0xd00d -> 1, 0xd00f -> 0, 0xd010 -> 3. Our lock sent
  only 0xd00c, AKSEvent 4, which the block ignores. So the lock never reached the one method that revokes the session.

- SweetPasscode.c, spc_lock_now_locked: when the lock actually happens (`l->unlocked` was true) it now raises
  SPC_EV_UNLOCKED as well as SPC_EV_LOCKED. SPC_EV_UNLOCKED is the event bit whose message is 0xd00d, the only form
  the interest block acts on; here it means "the lock state moved, re-read it", not "a session is open", and both
  consumers (SBFMobileKeyBag and MobileKeyBag) re-read the kernel's own answers and ignore the payload. The raise is
  inside the existing `if (l->unlocked)` guard, so a second selector 8/32/13 on an already locked phone posts
  nothing, and a lock with no code still does nothing at all. The event map in SweetKeyStore.cpp already sends
  0xd00c before 0xd00d, so the pair goes out in that order; the locked message is unchanged.
- SweetPasscode.h: the contract above, on spc_lock_now_locked's declaration.

Not changed: every selector's arguments and answers, the stub, the two-phase stage and commit, the device state
(`ss`, `sls`, `sb`, `sfa`, `sgs`, `sas`, `sgpe`, `srcd`), the entitlement, root, the backoff, and the
count-before-hash ordering. The kernel's post-lock answer was always sls 1; only its announcement was missing.

Host: check-2325.py runs springboard_test.c against the real SweetKeyStore.cpp. It now models the controller as well
as the keybag: _authState and the grace assertion (sb_auth_lock, sb_is_authenticated, sb_update_auth_for_lock_state,
sb_keypad_needed), the transient tracker's selector 26 hold, and the interest block's restriction to AKSEvent 1.
116 assertions in 11 tests (was 76 in 8). The three new tests are `lock needs code` (the ast266 defect end to end:
set, lock, selector 8, selector 26, then the lock screen's own question must say a keypad is needed),
`second lock quiet` and `lock without code`. 24 checks (was 20): 9 source, 14 negative controls and the blindness
control, each caught. The new controls remove 2326's half in the core and in the map, and add a guarded raise; each
makes the suite fail. The blindness control now also drops the three new tests before it rebuilds 2325's turn-off
test, and check-2325.py refuses a tree without 2326 by name instead of testing it as if it were the shipped one.
check-2phase 37, check-lock 57 (PASSCODE_OUT=out-lock3), test_turnon 21 cases unchanged.

Needs 2321, 2322, 2323, 2323b, 2324, 2325 and 2325b. Security code: plans/passcode/audit-brief-lock3.md is the audit
brief for the rebuilt image, and no image should boot until that audit is a GO.

